╭─ STRIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  An authorized white-box assessment of the Flask "orders" service at         │
│  `/workspace/test-vuln-hard` identified **three confirmed, remotely          │
│  exploitable injection vulnerabilities — one critical and two high** — with  │
│  working proof-of-concept exploits executed against a running instance of    │
│  the exact source.                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overall risk posture: Critical.** The application has no authentication,  │
│  no authorization, and no input validation on any of its three routes, and   │
│  every one of them hands attacker-controlled text to a dangerous sink.       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Key findings**                                                            │
│  - A critical server-side template injection in `POST /render` gives         │
│  unauthenticated remote code execution. Command output is returned directly  │
│  in the HTTP response, and four independent exploitation paths were          │
│  demonstrated, so no single code change in the template engine would         │
│  contain it.                                                                 │
│  - A high-severity SQL injection in `GET /orders?sort=` allows blind         │
│  extraction of the entire database through a boolean ordering oracle,        │
│  including a column the endpoint never projects.                             │
│  - A high-severity SQL injection in `POST /orders/by-sku` allows a single    │
│  request to dump every row of the table plus the schema definition.          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Business impact**                                                         │
│  - Complete application compromise: reading application secrets and          │
│  database contents, writing files, and pivoting to anything reachable from   │
│  the host.                                                                   │
│  - Confidentiality loss for all data the database account can read; the      │
│  application's own tracebacks additionally disclose internal source paths.   │
│  - The retained access log shows these same injection points were already    │
│  driven by automated tooling in practice, including a completed extraction   │
│  of a non-projectable column, so this is not a theoretical risk.             │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overarching remediation theme:** all three findings share one root cause  │
│  — user input is concatenated into a language that executes (SQL query       │
│  text, template source) instead of being passed as data. The fixes are       │
│  small and local: bound query parameters, a column allowlist for the         │
│  unavoidable `ORDER BY` case, and rendering user values as template *data*   │
│  rather than template *source*. Every fix was written, applied to a working  │
│  copy, and re-tested to confirm the exploit stops working while legitimate   │
│  behaviour is preserved.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  The assessment followed **OWASP WSTG** and **PTES** phases against a        │
│  single locally-hosted source tree, in a **white-box** mode: full source     │
│  review combined with live dynamic exploitation of a running instance of     │
│  that source.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Scope:** the Flask application `app.py`, its SQLite data store, and the   │
│  retained request log in the same directory. Testing was performed on        │
│  private working copies bound to test ports; the in-scope source files were  │
│  not modified, and the delivered patches are proposed through the reports    │
│  rather than applied in place.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Activities performed**                                                    │
│  - **Source mapping and static analysis:** a complete rule-based static      │
│  scan, a structural syntax-tree call map, a hardcoded-secret scan, and a     │
│  dependency/configuration scan. Three independent tools converged on         │
│  exactly the same three sinks, which establishes that the sink inventory is  │
│  complete.                                                                   │
│  - **Attack-surface enumeration:** exhaustive inventory of routes,           │
│  handlers, and framework registration mechanisms, including confirmation     │
│  that debug/console surfaces were not enabled.                               │
│  - **Dynamic exploitation:** hand-built proof-of-concept payloads for each   │
│  sink against a live instance, with matched benign baseline requests and     │
│  negative controls for every claim (including controls that distinguish the  │
│  actual template engine from the one the source comments claim).             │
│  - **Evidence corroboration:** review of the retained access log for         │
│  independent confirmation that the same injection points were exercised in   │
│  practice and for evidence of any additional exploited weakness.             │
│  - **Remediation and verification:** for each confirmed finding, a minimal   │
│  root-cause patch was derived, applied to a working copy, and re-tested —    │
│  confirming the exploit no longer succeeds, alternate payload classes are    │
│  covered, and the legitimate request/response behaviour is preserved.        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Constraints:** the target ships no automated test suite, linter           │
│  configuration, or CI, so repository-provided checks could not be run;       │
│  validity of each patch was instead established by compiling and             │
│  live-serving the patched module and exercising the full payload matrix.     │
│  This is recorded as an explicit verification gap on each finding.           │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Severity model:** ratings reflect demonstrated exploitability against a   │
│  live instance, with confidentiality, integrity, and availability set only   │
│  to the impact actually proven. Findings are: **1 Critical**, **2 High**.    │
│                                                                              │
│  **Confirmed findings**                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Critical — Server-side template injection to remote code execution,    │
│  `POST /render`** (CVSS 9.8). The `tpl` form field is passed as the          │
│  *template source* to `render_template_string`, so the submitted value is    │
│  compiled by the running Jinja2 environment instead of being rendered as     │
│  data. A benign submission returns as plain text, while `{{7*7}}` returns    │
│  `49`; escalation through framework globals reaches `os.popen(...)`, and     │
│  the command output is returned in the response body (identity, host kernel  │
│  banner, and contents of a file created outside the application were all     │
│  retrieved). Four independent gadget chains succeeded, confirming there is   │
│  no sandbox. The source comments describe the engine as Mako, which is       │
│  incorrect and would have misdirected remediation; runtime fingerprinting    │
│  identified Jinja2. Read-only, authentication-free, no user interaction.     │
│                                                                              │
│  2. **High — Blind SQL injection in `GET /orders?sort=`** (CVSS 7.5). The    │
│  `sort` parameter is interpolated into an `ORDER BY` clause. Because the     │
│  injection point is an expression position, a `UNION` is rejected by the     │
│  database grammar; the effective technique is a conditional expression that  │
│  flips the result ordering between true and false. That ordering difference  │
│  is a stable boolean oracle, and binary search recovered the complete        │
│  contents of a column the endpoint never projects, the database version,     │
│  and schema metadata. Integrity and availability impact were not             │
│  demonstrated and are scored accordingly.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  3. **High — SQL injection in `POST /orders/by-sku`** (CVSS 7.5). The JSON   │
│  body field `sku` is interpolated into a single-quoted string literal. A     │
│  single request terminates the literal, discards the intended predicate,     │
│  and unions the whole table — returning every row with its `note` values     │
│  against a one-row benign baseline — and further requests retrieved the      │
│  database version, aggregated data, and the table definition. Delivered as   │
│  an ordinary JSON body, so query-string-only filtering would not have        │
│  helped.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **Systemic root cause:** all three defects are the same class of error at   │
│  three different sinks — untrusted text is placed into a language that is    │
│  later *interpreted* (SQL query text, template source) rather than being     │
│  passed through a data channel. The codebase applies no allowlisting, no     │
│  parameter binding, and no separation of template from data anywhere.        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Compensating controls found:** essentially none. Debug mode is disabled,  │
│  which removes the framework debugger console as an additional path, but     │
│  tracebacks for unhandled database errors still disclose internal source     │
│  paths. The only mitigations that bear on reach are deployment-level         │
│  (loopback binding in the lab configuration) and do not change the           │
│  weakness.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  **Areas assessed and cleared**                                              │
│  - The route inventory is closed: three routes only, no blueprints, no       │
│  custom error handlers, no dynamic route registration.                       │
│  - Three independent static analysis techniques produced no sink outside     │
│  the three reported, including the absence of any command execution,         │
│  deserialization, file handling, or path construction.                       │
│  - Hardcoded-secret scanning produced no real credentials; the one apparent  │
│  flag is a deliberate planted test marker, and other hits are synthetic      │
│  probe URLs embedded in the log.                                             │
│  - Dependency analysis does not apply: the target ships no manifest,         │
│  lockfile, or container definition, and the installed framework versions     │
│  are current.                                                                │
│  - The retained access log shows no additional weakness was successfully     │
│  exercised: no unexpected successful request to any non-application path,    │
│  no successful file read, no code-execution artifact, and no credential      │
│  disclosure. The tens of thousands of logged error tracebacks are a side     │
│  effect of the three reported sinks, not an independent issue.               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Attack-chaining assessment:** chaining was considered and does not        │
│  elevate the rating. The template injection already provides code execution  │
│  as the service account, so it can read the application's data store and     │
│  configuration directly — a stronger primitive than either SQL injection,    │
│  which cannot execute code. Nor do the two SQL injections chain to anything  │
│  beyond the database read they already demonstrate: the data store is a      │
│  local file, so the SQL layer provides no path to the template sink or to    │
│  the operating system. The findings are therefore independent, and the       │
│  critical rating of the template injection already reflects the terminal     │
│  impact an attacker would reach.                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Immediate (within days)**                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Fix the template injection first** — it is unauthenticated remote      │
│  code execution. Stop treating request input as template *source*: use a     │
│  fixed, developer-controlled template and pass the submitted value in as a   │
│  context variable so it can only be rendered as data. Do not attempt to      │
│  solve this with payload filtering or blocklists. If a user-facing template  │
│  editor is a genuine product requirement, that separate feature needs a      │
│  sandboxed template environment with output autoescaping and an explicit     │
│  allowlist of available objects and functions, and should be independently   │
│  reviewed before exposure.                                                   │
│  2. **Bind parameters on `POST /orders/by-sku`** — replace the               │
│  string-literal interpolation with a positional placeholder and a value      │
│  tuple. This is a one-line change that eliminates the entire injection       │
│  class on that route.                                                        │
│  3. **Allowlist the `GET /orders` sort key** — column names cannot be bound  │
│  as parameters, so map the accepted public sort keys to fixed, hard-coded    │
│  SQL fragments through a module-level constant and reject anything else      │
│  with a client error before the query is built.                              │
│  4. **Correct the misleading source documentation** that misidentifies the   │
│  template engine; security guidance and detection rules written against the  │
│  wrong engine will not protect the service.                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Short-term (within a release)**                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  5. **Sweep for the same pattern everywhere.** Grep for string formatting,   │
│  concatenation, and `%` interpolation inside query strings and template      │
│  compilation, and remove every instance. Add a lint rule or static-analysis  │
│  gate that fails the build on interpolated SQL.                              │
│  6. **Add regression tests for each fix** asserting that: a quote-and-union  │
│  payload returns no rows; an injection-style sort key returns a rejection    │
│  response rather than data; and a template-expression payload is rendered    │
│  literally. Tests must be written so they fail against the vulnerable code.  │
│  7. **Stop leaking internal detail on error.** The database error            │
│  tracebacks disclose source paths; return a generic error response and log   │
│  the detail server-side only.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Medium-term (hardening)**                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  8. **Introduce authentication and authorization** on all routes — the       │
│  entire application is presently open to anyone who can reach the port.      │
│  Even with the injections fixed, an unauthenticated data endpoint is an      │
│  unnecessary exposure.                                                       │
│  9. **Apply least privilege at the data layer:** run the service with a      │
│  database account or file permissions limited to the tables and operations   │
│  it actually needs, and open connections read-only where the route only      │
│  reads.                                                                      │
│  10. **Reduce blast radius of any future code execution:** run the service   │
│  as a dedicated unprivileged account in an isolated environment with egress  │
│  restrictions, and keep the debugger and interactive consoles disabled in    │
│  every environment.                                                          │
│  11. **Bind to loopback or a management network** unless the service is      │
│  intended to be publicly reachable, and place a request-size and rate limit  │
│  in front of the rendering route.                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  **Retest and validation**                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  After remediation, re-run the original payload set against the patched      │
│  build to confirm: the template-expression payloads render literally with    │
│  no command output; the conditional-ordering oracle returns no true/false    │
│  distinction; the union payload returns an empty result; and all legitimate  │
│  requests return the same responses, in the same shape, as before the        │
│  change. Also confirm the three new regression tests fail when reverted,     │
│  proving they actually guard the fixes.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
